= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <absolute value> is nonnegative, so a real point must satisfy $1-x^2\geq0$, or $-1\leq x\leq1$. On that interval the two branches are
$$
\boxed{y=1-x^2\quad\text{or}\quad y=x^2-1,\qquad -1\leq x\leq1.}
$$
They form a closed lens symmetric about both coordinate axes, meeting at $(\pm1,0)$ and reaching $(0,\pm1)$. The <parabolas> outside the stated interval do not belong to the original <locus>.

\Image[paper-1-absolute-value.png]
{title=The absolute-value locus and its intersections with the horizontal line y = 1/2}

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substituting $y=1/2$ into the <absolute value> relation gives $1/2=1-x^2$, so $x^2=1/2$. All real intersections are
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(\frac1{\sqrt2},\frac12\right),\quad
\left(-\frac1{\sqrt2},\frac12\right).}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Separate variables near the nonzero <initial condition>: $y^{-2}dy=dx$. <Integration> gives $-1/y=x+C$, and $y(1)=1$ fixes $C=-2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac1{2-x},\qquad -\infty<x<2.}
$$
This is the maximal connected interval containing the initial point. Direct <differentiation> gives $y'=1/(2-x)^2=y^2$; the pole at $x=2$ prevents continuation as a finite classical <solution of a differential equation> through that point.

