= Solution
{parent=8}

The base $2e$ is constant. Write the <exponential function> as $\exp[\cos x\ln(2e)]$ and apply the <chain rule>:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}(2e)^{\cos x}
=-(1+\ln2)\sin x\,(2e)^{\cos x}.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Rewrite the <rational function> as $y=1-1/(x^3+1)$. It is undefined at $x=-1$, with the vertical <asymptote> $x=-1$, and tends to the horizontal <asymptote> $y=1$ as $x\to\pm\infty$. At the pole, $y\to+\infty$ from the left and $y\to-\infty$ from the right.

Its <derivative> is
$$
y'=\frac{3x^2}{(x^3+1)^2}\geq0,
$$
vanishing only at $x=0$. Since $y(0)=0$ and $y=x^3+O(x^6)$ near zero, this is a stationary <inflection point>, not an extremum. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{stationary point }(0,0);\qquad\text{asymptotes }x=-1,\ y=1.}
$$
The graph increases on each side of the vertical <asymptote>. The left branch lies above $y=1$, while the right branch lies below it and crosses the origin horizontally.

\Image[paper-1-rational-graph.png]
{title=The rational-function graph, its two asymptotes, and its stationary inflection at the origin}

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The first curve is a <circle> of <radius> $1$ centred at $(2,1)$. A line through the origin with equation $kx-y=0$ intersects it precisely when the perpendicular distance from that <centre> is at most the <radius>. The <distance from a point to a line> gives
$$
\frac{|2k-1|}{\sqrt{k^2+1}}\leq1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
(2k-1)^2\leq k^2+1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad k(3k-4)\leq0.
$$
Hence the full parameter range is
$$
\boxed{0\leq k\leq\frac43.}
$$
The two endpoint lines are tangent, at $(2,0)$ and $(6/5,8/5)$ respectively; interior values give two real intersections. Both endpoints are therefore included.

