= Solution
{parent=a}

The original PDF places a <complex modulus> around the quotient; those bars are essential. Its denominator excludes $z=-i$. Using $|z^*-i|=|z+i|$, the case $\lambda=1$ becomes $|z-i|=|z+i|$. In Cartesian coordinates,
$$
x^2+(y-1)^2=x^2+(y+1)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\boxed{y=0.}
$$
This <locus> is the real axis, the perpendicular bisector of the points $i$ and $-i$ in the <complex plane>. The excluded denominator point does not lie on it.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A quotient with nonzero denominator has zero <complex modulus> exactly when its numerator is zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{z=i,\quad\text{a single point}.}
$$
At that point $z^*-i=-2i\ne0$, so the solution is admissible.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For real $\lambda>0$, squaring the two nonnegative moduli gives $|z-i|^2=\lambda^2|z+i|^2$. In Cartesian coordinates this is
$$
(1-\lambda^2)(x^2+y^2+1)-2(1+\lambda^2)y=0.
$$
Assume $\lambda\ne1$ and put $h=(1+\lambda^2)/(1-\lambda^2)$. Dividing by $1-\lambda^2$ yields $x^2+y^2-2hy+1=0$. Since $zz^*=x^2+y^2$ and $bz^*+b^*z=2\operatorname{Re}(bz^*)$, the requested complex equation has
$$
\boxed{b=-i\frac{1+\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^2},\qquad c=1.}
$$
Indeed these choices make the mixed term $-2hy$. Completing the square gives
$$
x^2+(y-h)^2=h^2-1=\frac{4\lambda^2}{(1-\lambda^2)^2}.
$$
The <circles of Apollonius> therefore have
$$
\boxed{\text{centre }z_c=i\frac{1+\lambda^2}{1-\lambda^2},\qquad
\text{radius }\frac{2\lambda}{|1-\lambda^2|}.}
$$
The <radius> is positive for the stated range. No spurious point at $z=-i$ has been introduced: at that point the undivided equation would require $4=0$. Positive $\lambda<1$ gives a <circle> above the real axis, while $\lambda>1$ gives one below it.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For arbitrary complex $b$ and real $c$, complete the <complex modulus> square:
$$
zz^*+bz^*+b^*z+c=|z+b|^2+c-|b|^2.
$$
The <locus> is a single point exactly when
$$
\boxed{c=|b|^2,\qquad z=-b.}
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The same completed square gives $|z+b|^2=|b|^2-c$. A nondegenerate <circle> therefore occurs exactly when
$$
\boxed{c<|b|^2,\qquad\text{centre }-b,\quad\text{radius }\sqrt{|b|^2-c}.}
$$
For completeness, $c>|b|^2$ gives an empty <locus> because a squared <complex modulus> cannot be negative.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No finite choice of $b$ and $c$ gives a line.** The coefficient of $zz^*=x^2+y^2$ is fixed at one, so completing the square always gives a <circle>, a point, or the empty set. A general equation $Azz^*+bz^*+b^*z+c=0$ could describe a line when $A=0$ and $b\ne0$, but changing the fixed quadratic coefficient is not permitted here. A limiting sequence of increasingly large <circles> does not make any one of these finite equations a line.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $f(x,y)=F(u,v)$ in the proposed local coordinates. The <multivariable chain rule> gives
$$
f_x=2xF_u+2yF_v,\qquad f_y=-2yF_u+2xF_v.
$$
Consequently
$$
yf_x+xf_y=2(x^2+y^2)F_v=0.
$$
Away from the origin the <Jacobian determinant> is $u_xv_y-u_yv_x=4(x^2+y^2)>0$, so the <inverse function theorem> justifies these local coordinates. Therefore $F_v=0$ and the local <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=\Phi(x^2-y^2).}
$$
Substitution verifies the result: $f_x=2x\Phi'$ and $f_y=-2y\Phi'$, whose weighted sum vanishes.

There is a global qualification. The coordinate map is two-to-one, and a <level set> of $x^2-y^2$ has separate <characteristic curve> branches. A single profile on the entire plane does not follow without a branch-identification assumption. For example, take $f=\exp[-1/(x^2-y^2)^2]$ on the wedge $x>0$, $x^2>y^2$, and zero elsewhere. This is smooth, satisfies the equation, and has different values at $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ despite equal $u$. The <characteristic components of a hyperbolic first-order equation> explain this obstruction. At the origin the coordinate map is singular and the original equation reads $0=0$; the boxed answer is the intended local form on a regular branch, or a global form under an additional identification of branches.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first <partial derivatives> factor as
$$
g_x=4x(2+x^2-3y^2),\qquad g_y=4y(2+y^2-3x^2).
$$
If either coordinate vanishes, the other equation forces the other coordinate to vanish as well. If both are nonzero, subtracting the two bracket equations gives $x^2=y^2$, and then $x^2=y^2=1$. Thus the complete list of <stationary points> is $(0,0)$ and the four points $(\pm1,\pm1)$.

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H_g=\begin{pmatrix}8+12x^2-12y^2&-24xy\\-24xy&8+12y^2-12x^2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin it is $8I$, which is a <positive-definite matrix>. At the four other points its <eigenvalues> are $32$ and $-16$, independently of the signs. The <second-derivative test> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{(0,0):\text{ strict local minimum, }g=0;\qquad
(\pm1,\pm1):\text{ saddle points, }g=4.}
$$
There are no <local maxima>. The minimum at the origin is not global, since $g(t,t)=8t^2-4t^4\to-\infty$ as $|t|\to\infty$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Within the requested first-quadrant square, the <stationary points> visible in the <contour plot> are the corner minimum $(0,0)$ and the saddle $(1,1)$. Near the origin, $g\simeq4(x^2+y^2)$, so the low positive contours look like quarter-circles. Along either coordinate axis, $g=4t^2+t^4$ rises monotonically. Along the diagonal, $g(t,t)=8t^2-4t^4$ rises to $4$ at $t=1$, returns to zero at $t=\sqrt2$, then becomes negative.

The saddle contour $g=4$ crosses at $(1,1)$ and separates the central positive contours from the outer diagonal region. Negative contours occur toward $(2,2)$, where $g=-32$, while the axis corners $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ have $g=32$. These checks determine both the contour ordering and the change of connectivity at the saddle; the figure shows these level sets.

\Image[paper-1-quartic-contours.png]
{title=Contours of the quartic function in the first-quadrant square, with the saddle level highlighted}

