= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\mathbf a=(a_x,a_y,a_z)$. The <cross product> gives $\mathbf v_1=(a_yz-a_zy,a_zx-a_xz,a_xy-a_yx)$, whose three diagonal <partial derivatives> vanish. For the second <vector field>, direct <differentiation> gives $\partial_x(2xy)=2y$, $\partial_y(x^2+3y^2+z^2)=6y$, and $\partial_z(2yz)=2y$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf v_1=0,\qquad
\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf v_2=10y.}
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $L=5\pi/2$. The curve tangent is $\mathbf r'=(-b\sin\theta,b\cos\theta,c)$. For the first <line integral>, the <scalar triple product> identity gives $(\mathbf a\times\mathbf r)\cdot\mathbf r'=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf r\times\mathbf r')$. Its <cross product> is
$$
\mathbf r\times\mathbf r'=\bigl(bc(\sin\theta-\theta\cos\theta),\ -bc(\theta\sin\theta+\cos\theta),\ b^2\bigr).
$$
The needed <definite integrals> are
$$
\int_0^L(\sin\theta-\theta\cos\theta)d\theta
=\big[-\theta\sin\theta-2\cos\theta\big]_0^L=2-L,
$$
and $\int_0^L(-\theta\sin\theta-\cos\theta)d\theta=[\theta\cos\theta-2\sin\theta]_0^L=-2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{I_1=bc\left(2-\frac{5\pi}{2}\right)a_x-2bc\,a_y+\frac{5\pi b^2}{2}a_z.}
$$
For the second <line integral>, substitute the curve into $\mathbf v_2\cdot\mathbf r'$. The cubic terms combine to $b^3\cos\theta$, leaving
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf v_2\cdot\mathbf r'
&=b^3\cos\theta+bc^2\theta^2\cos\theta+2bc^2\theta\sin\theta\\
&=\frac d{d\theta}\left[b\sin\theta\,(b^2+c^2\theta^2)\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
The <fundamental theorem of calculus> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{I_2=b\left(b^2+\frac{25\pi^2c^2}{4}\right).}
$$
Both calculations retain the full path from $\theta=0$ to $5\pi/2$, rather than inadvertently closing the <helix> after one turn.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Direct calculation of the <curl> gives
$$
\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v_1=2\mathbf a,\qquad
\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf v_2=\mathbf0.
$$
Since $\mathbf a\ne0$, the first <vector field> cannot be a <gradient field>: the <curl of a gradient> is zero. The second is a smooth <polynomial> field on all of the <simply connected> space $\mathbb R^3$, so its vanishing <curl> makes it a <conservative vector field>. A potential is also constructed explicitly in the next part. Thus **$\mathbf v_2$ is a <conservative vector field> and $\mathbf v_1$ is not**. A vanishing <divergence> alone, as for $\mathbf v_1$, is a different property and does not establish conservativity.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Integrating $\phi_x=2xy$ gives $\phi=x^2y+h(y,z)$. Matching $\phi_y=x^2+3y^2+z^2$ then gives $h=y^3+yz^2+k(z)$. Finally $\phi_z=2yz$ forces $k$ to be constant. A <scalar potential> is
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y,z)=x^2y+y^3+yz^2+C.}
$$
The initial and final points of the <helix> are $(b,0,0)$ and $(0,b,cL)$, where $L=5\pi/2$. The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> gives
$$
\phi(0,b,cL)-\phi(b,0,0)=b^3+bc^2L^2
=\boxed{b\left(b^2+\frac{25\pi^2c^2}{4}\right)},
$$
agreeing with the directly evaluated $I_2$.

