= Solution
{parent=i}

By the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>, choose the normalized <eigenvectors> to form an **<orthonormal basis>**. With $P_n=\mathbf e_n\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}$, orthonormality gives $P_nP_m=\delta_{nm}P_n$ and $\sum_nP_n=I$. For every vector $\mathbf x$, expand $\mathbf x=\sum_n(\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}\mathbf x)\mathbf e_n$ and apply $A$:
$$
A\mathbf x=\sum_n\lambda_n\mathbf e_n(\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}\mathbf x).
$$
Since this equality holds for every $\mathbf x$, the <spectral decomposition> is
$$
\boxed{A=\sum_{n=1}^N\lambda_n\mathbf e_n\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}.}
$$
Each $P_n$ is the rank-one <orthogonal projection> onto its <eigenvector> direction. In a degenerate <eigenspace>, normalized <eigenvectors> must be chosen mutually orthogonal; normalization alone does not guarantee this resolution of the identity.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <matrix trace> is linear, and
$$
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf e_n\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T})
=\sum_j(e_n)_j^2=\|\mathbf e_n\|^2=1.
$$
Taking the <trace> of the <spectral decomposition> therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}A=\sum_{n=1}^N\lambda_n.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If every <eigenvalue> is nonzero, define $B=\sum_n\lambda_n^{-1}P_n$, where $P_n=\mathbf e_n\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}$. Using the mutually <orthogonal projection> identity $P_nP_m=\delta_{nm}P_n$,
$$
AB=\sum_{n,m}\frac{\lambda_n}{\lambda_m}P_nP_m=\sum_nP_n=I.
$$
The same calculation gives $BA=I$. Thus the <matrix inverse> is
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\sum_{n=1}^N\frac1{\lambda_n}\mathbf e_n\mathbf e_n^{\mathsf T}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The inverse fails to exist exactly when **at least one <eigenvalue> is zero**. In that case a nonzero <eigenvector> lies in the <kernel of a linear map>, so $A$ is not one-to-one. Equivalently, its <determinant> $\prod_n\lambda_n$ is zero. Conversely, if every <eigenvalue> is nonzero, the preceding construction gives an inverse.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The third coordinate is decoupled, giving the unit <eigenvector> $(0,0,1)^{\mathsf T}$ with <eigenvalue> $1$. In the first two coordinates, the symmetric direction $(1,1)$ has <eigenvalue> $3-1=2$, and the antisymmetric direction $(1,-1)$ has <eigenvalue> $3+1=4$. Their normalized forms are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda_1=2&\mathbf e_1=\dfrac1{\sqrt2}(1,1,0)^{\mathsf T}\\[3pt]
\lambda_2=4&\mathbf e_2=\dfrac1{\sqrt2}(1,-1,0)^{\mathsf T}\\[3pt]
\lambda_3=1&\mathbf e_3=(0,0,1)^{\mathsf T}.
\end{array}}
$$
They are an <orthonormal basis> of $\mathbb R^3$; independent sign reversals are equally valid choices.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Insert the <eigenvalues> and <eigenvectors> into the inverse <spectral decomposition>. The first two projections contribute
$$
\frac12\mathbf e_1\mathbf e_1^{\mathsf T}+\frac14\mathbf e_2\mathbf e_2^{\mathsf T}
=\begin{pmatrix}3/8&1/8&0\\1/8&3/8&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Adding the third projection gives
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}3/8&1/8&0\\1/8&3/8&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication by $A$ gives the identity; in particular the upper block satisfies $\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\-1&3\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\1&3\end{pmatrix}=8I$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Summing the diagonal entries gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1})=\frac38+\frac38+1=\frac74.}
$$
The <eigenvalues> of the inverse are the reciprocals $1/2,1/4,1$ of those of $A$, whose sum is also $7/4$. This is precisely what the <trace> identity predicts.

