{parent=i}

Assume the needed <derivatives> exist continuously and $df/dg\ne0$ on a neighborhood, so the <inverse function theorem> provides a local inverse. Put $p=df/dg$, $q=d^2f/dg^2$, and $r=d^3f/dg^3$. The <chain rule> gives $d/df=p^{-1}d/dg$, so
$$
\frac{dg}{df}=\frac1p,\qquad
\frac{d^2g}{df^2}=\frac1p\frac d{dg}\frac1p=-\frac q{p^3}.
$$
Differentiate once more:
$$
\frac{d^3g}{df^3}=\frac1p\frac d{dg}(-qp^{-3})
=-\frac r{p^4}+\frac{3q^2}{p^5}.
$$
Thus the <first three derivatives of an inverse function> are
$$
\boxed{\frac{dg}{df}=\frac1{df/dg},\qquad
\frac{d^2g}{df^2}=-\frac{d^2f/dg^2}{(df/dg)^3},\qquad
\frac{d^3g}{df^3}=
\frac{3(d^2f/dg^2)^2-(df/dg)(d^3f/dg^3)}{(df/dg)^5}.}
$$
All forward <derivatives> on the right are evaluated at the corresponding value $g(f)$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $a_1=f'(0)\ne0$, substitute the proposed inverse series into the forward one. Comparing coefficients of $f,f^2,f^3$ gives
$$
a_1b_1=1,\qquad a_1b_2+a_2b_1^2=0,\qquad
a_1b_3+2a_2b_1b_2+a_3b_1^3=0.
$$
Solving successively yields the <cubic series reversion> coefficients
$$
\boxed{b_1=\frac1{a_1},\qquad b_2=-\frac{a_2}{a_1^3},\qquad
b_3=\frac{2a_2^2-a_1a_3}{a_1^5}.}
$$
The conditions $f(0)=0$ and $a_1\ne0$ ensure that both local series are centred at zero and that the inverse has a linear leading term.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The arrival phase has a <uniform distribution> over a cycle of length $R$. The no-delay phases occupy length $R-r$, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(S=0)=\frac{R-r}{R}=1-\frac rR.}
$$
Individual phase endpoints have <probability> zero. The model neglects clearance time and releases waiting vehicles together when the stop interval ends; the degenerate case $r=R$ is interpreted as release at successive cycle boundaries.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a stop-phase arrival at time $t$, the delay is $s=r-t$. An interval of delays of width $ds$ therefore corresponds to an interval of arrival phases of the same width, because $|dt/ds|=1$. Its unconditional <probability> is $ds/R$. Hence the positive-delay part has density
$$
\boxed{p(s)=\begin{cases}1/R,&0<s\leq r,\\0,&r<s\leq R.\end{cases}}
$$
This ordinary density integrates to $r/R$, not to one: the remaining <probability mass> is at $s=0$. Thus $S$ has a <uniform distribution with an atom at zero>, with mixing weight $q=r/R$. Conditional on a positive delay, the density would instead be $1/r$ on $(0,r)$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The graph of the positive-delay <probability density function> is a horizontal segment at height $1/R$ from $s=0$ to $r$, followed by zero from $r$ to $R$. Values at individual endpoints do not affect <probabilities>. The separate atom $1-r/R$ at zero is not a finite-height part of this density graph.

\Image[paper-1-delay-distribution.png]
{title=Positive-delay density and the full cumulative distribution, whose jump records the no-delay atom}

The companion <cumulative distribution function> in the figure is $F_S(s)=0$ for $s<0$, $F_S(s)=1-r/R+s/R$ for $0\leq s<r$, and $F_S(s)=1$ for $s\geq r$. Its jump at zero displays the discrete part without confusing density height with <probability mass>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <probability> of a positive delay is the integral of its density:
$$
\mathbb P(S>0)=\int_0^r\frac{ds}{R}=\frac rR.
$$
Adding the separate no-delay event gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(S=0)+\mathbb P(S>0)=\left(1-\frac rR\right)+\frac rR=1.}
$$
This is the <normalization of a probability distribution> with both discrete and continuous parts.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The atom at zero contributes zero to the <expected value>, while all positive delays must retain their unconditional density. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mu=\mathbb E[S]=0\left(1-\frac rR\right)+\int_0^r\frac{s}{R}\,ds=\frac{r^2}{2R}.}
$$
This <mean> is $r/R$ times the <mean> $r/2$ conditional on encountering a stop; using $r/2$ alone would omit the vehicles passing without delay.

= vi
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The second <moment> is
$$
\mathbb E[S^2]=\int_0^r\frac{s^2}{R}\,ds=\frac{r^3}{3R}.
$$
Subtracting the squared <expected value> gives the <variance> and <standard deviation>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(S)=\frac{r^3}{3R}-\frac{r^4}{4R^2}
=\frac{r^3(4R-3r)}{12R^2},\qquad
\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{r^3(4R-3r)}{12R^2}}.}
$$
The <variance> is nonnegative because $0<r\leq R$. Both $\mu$ and $\sigma$ have units of time. At $r=R$ the result reduces to $R/\sqrt{12}$, the <standard deviation> of a <uniform distribution> over one full cycle.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Condition throughout on the given arrival time $t$ of A, with $0\leq t<r$. A departs at time $r$. B also departs at $r$ exactly when it arrives before that release, so $t+\tau_0\leq r$, or $0<\tau_0\leq r-t$. Since $\tau_0$ is uniform on an interval of length $R$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\tau_1=0\mid t)=\frac{r-t}{R}.}
$$
The original timeline below shows the three disjoint ranges of $\tau_0$. The same idealized simultaneous-release convention as in part (a) is used.

\Image[paper-1-arrival-timeline.png]
{title=Conditional arrival ranges for the following vehicle at a fixed arrival phase of the leading vehicle}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If B arrives during the remaining pass phase, then $r<t+\tau_0<R$, or $r-t<\tau_0<R-t$. It passes immediately, so
$$
\tau_1=(t+\tau_0)-r=\tau_0-(r-t).
$$
Because $r-t>0$, this is strictly between zero and $\tau_0$. The phase interval has length $(R-t)-(r-t)=R-r$, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(0<\tau_1<\tau_0\mid t)=\frac{R-r}{R}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The remaining arrivals occur in the next stop phase: $R\leq t+\tau_0\leq R+t$, or $R-t\leq\tau_0\leq R$. B waits until $R+r$, while A departed at $r$, giving $\tau_1=R\geq\tau_0$. This interval has length $t$, hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\tau_1\geq\tau_0\mid t)=\frac tR.}
$$
Endpoint assignments do not affect <probabilities>. The three outcomes are exhaustive and satisfy
$$
\boxed{\frac{r-t}{R}+\frac{R-r}{R}+\frac tR=1.}
$$
These are <conditional probabilities> for the stated fixed $t$; there is no additional averaging over A's arrival phase in the question.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Integrating $y''=0$ once gives a constant first <derivative>, and integrating again gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_0+C_1x.}
$$
There are two independent constants, as expected for a second-order <ordinary differential equation>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

After $n$ integrations, the <general solution> is an arbitrary <polynomial> of degree at most $n-1$:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}C_jx^j.}
$$
Conversely, every such <polynomial> has zero $n$th <derivative>. The $n$ free coefficients span the full solution space on a connected interval.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the homogeneous equation, substitute the <exponential function> $e^{kx}$. The <characteristic equation> is
$$
k^5-k=k(k^4-1)=k(k-1)(k+1)(k^2+1)=0.
$$
Its distinct roots are $0,1,-1,i,-i$. Combining the complex-conjugate pair into real <sine> and <cosine> gives the <complementary function>
$$
\boxed{y_c=C_0+C_1e^x+C_2e^{-x}+C_3\cos x+C_4\sin x.}
$$
Five linearly independent terms supply the required five homogeneous degrees of freedom.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A quadratic <polynomial> is sufficient: set $y_p=Ax^2$. Its fifth <derivative> vanishes, so $y_p^{(5)}-y_p'=-2Ax$. Matching the forcing gives $A=-1/2$, hence a <particular integral> is
$$
\boxed{y_p=-\frac{x^2}{2}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Add the <particular solution> to the <complementary function>. The real <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y=C_0+C_1e^x+C_2e^{-x}+C_3\cos x+C_4\sin x-\frac{x^2}{2}.}
$$
The five constants remain arbitrary until initial data are specified.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Impose the five <initial conditions> at zero on the preceding <general solution>. They give
$$
\begin{aligned}
C_0+C_1+C_2+C_3&=1,\\
C_1-C_2+C_4&=0,\\
C_1+C_2-C_3-1&=0,\\
C_1-C_2-C_4&=0,\\
C_1+C_2+C_3&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
The second and fourth equations give $C_1=C_2$ and $C_4=0$. The third and fifth then yield $C_1=C_2=1/4$ and $C_3=-1/2$, while the first gives $C_0=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=1+\frac14(e^x+e^{-x})-\frac12\cos x-\frac{x^2}{2}
=1+\frac12(\cosh x-\cos x)-\frac{x^2}{2}.}
$$
For a direct check, the difference of its fifth and first <derivatives> is $x$. At zero its value is one; its first, second, third and fourth <derivatives> vanish. In particular the quadratic term cancels the otherwise nonzero second <derivative> of the hyperbolic-cosine and <cosine> combination.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Partition the finite interval as $a=x_0<x_1<\cdots<x_N=b$, with $\Delta x_i=x_{i+1}-x_i$. For a <Riemann integrable> <function>, the left-endpoint <Riemann sums> satisfy
$$
\boxed{\int_a^b f(x)\,dx
=\lim_{\max_i\Delta x_i\to0}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}f(x_i)\Delta x_i.}
$$
The maximum subinterval width, rather than merely the number of subintervals, must tend to zero. Continuity of $f$ on the closed interval is a sufficient integrability hypothesis. More generally, any chosen sample point in each subinterval gives the same <definite integral> under these assumptions.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Index the stated <geometric progression> by $x_i=(1+\epsilon)^i$, $i=0,1,\ldots$. Then $\Delta x_i=\epsilon(1+\epsilon)^i$, and its left-endpoint contribution is
$$
f(x_i)\Delta x_i=(1+\epsilon)^{-(1+\alpha)i}\epsilon(1+\epsilon)^i
=\epsilon\big[(1+\epsilon)^{-\alpha}\big]^i.
$$
Hence the approximation is a <geometric series> with
$$
\boxed{\text{first term }\epsilon,\qquad q=(1+\epsilon)^{-\alpha},\qquad
U_\epsilon=\frac{\epsilon}{1-(1+\epsilon)^{-\alpha}}.}
$$
Since $\epsilon>0$ and $\alpha>0$, the ratio satisfies $0<q<1$ and the series converges. The integrand is decreasing, so the left-endpoint sum is an upper bound for the <improper integral>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <binomial series> gives $(1+\epsilon)^{-\alpha}=1-\alpha\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2)$. Therefore
$$
U_\epsilon=\frac{\epsilon}{\alpha\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2)}\longrightarrow\frac1\alpha.
$$
To justify the limiting approximation over the infinite interval, also use the right-endpoint sum. It is $L_\epsilon=(1+\epsilon)^{-1-\alpha}U_\epsilon$, and monotonicity gives $L_\epsilon\leq I\leq U_\epsilon$. Both bounds have the same limit. The <geometric partition bounds for a power-law improper integral> and the <squeeze theorem> thus prove
$$
\boxed{I=\frac1\alpha,\qquad\alpha>0.}
$$
This bounding argument matters because the geometric partition has unbounded widths far along the half-line; a finite-interval maximum-mesh argument cannot simply be applied to the entire infinite partition at fixed $\epsilon$.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $m=2$, evaluate the <improper integral> directly:
$$
I_{2,n}=\int_0^\infty(1+x)^{-n}dx
=\left[\frac{(1+x)^{1-n}}{1-n}\right]_0^\infty
=\boxed{\frac1{n-1}}.
$$
For $m\geq3$, use <integration by parts> with $u=x^{m-2}$ and $dv=(1+x)^{-(m+n-2)}dx$. Then $v=-(1+x)^{-(m+n-3)}/(m+n-3)$ and
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{m,n}
&=\left[-\frac{x^{m-2}}{(m+n-3)(1+x)^{m+n-3}}\right]_0^\infty\\
&\quad+\frac{m-2}{m+n-3}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^{m-3}}{(1+x)^{m+n-3}}dx\\
&=\boxed{\frac{m-2}{m+n-3}I_{m-1,n}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The boundary term vanishes: at zero $m-2>0$, while at infinity its power is $1-n<0$. The restriction $m\geq3$ belongs to this recurrence step; the separate $m=2$ base case must not be replaced by a formal zero times a divergent $I_{1,n}$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply the recurrence repeatedly down to $m=2$:
$$
I_{m,n}=\frac{(m-2)(m-3)\cdots1}{(m+n-3)(m+n-4)\cdots n}\,I_{2,n}.
$$
Including the base factor $1/(n-1)$ and rewriting the products as <factorials> gives
$$
\boxed{I_{m,n}=\frac{(m-2)!(n-2)!}{(m+n-3)!},\qquad m,n\in\mathbb Z,\quad m,n>1.}
$$
The formula also includes $m=2$ because $0!=1$. It is symmetric in $m,n$, consistent with the <integration by substitution> $x=1/t$. Equivalently it is the <Euler beta function> $B(m-1,n-1)$, although that identification is not needed for the recurrence derivation.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <multivariable chain rule> to an arbitrary differentiable scalar <function> of $x,y,z$. With the subscripts indicating the coordinates held fixed, the first operator is
$$
\boxed{(\partial_W)_{\theta,\phi}
=\tan\theta\cos\phi\,(\partial_x)_{y,z}
+\tan\theta\sin\phi\,(\partial_y)_{x,z}
+(\partial_z)_{x,y}.}
$$
Holding $W,\phi$ fixed when differentiating with respect to $\theta$ gives
$$
\boxed{(\partial_\theta)_{W,\phi}
=W\sec^2\theta\cos\phi\,(\partial_x)_{y,z}
+W\sec^2\theta\sin\phi\,(\partial_y)_{x,z}.}
$$
Finally, holding $W,\theta$ fixed gives
$$
\boxed{(\partial_\phi)_{W,\theta}
=-W\tan\theta\sin\phi\,(\partial_x)_{y,z}
+W\tan\theta\cos\phi\,(\partial_y)_{x,z}.}
$$
For example, the coefficient of $\partial_x$ in $\partial_\theta$ is precisely $(\partial x/\partial\theta)_{W,\phi}$; all coefficients follow by differentiating the given coordinate <functions>.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Suppress the held-fixed subscripts only within this matrix display. Collecting the preceding operator coefficients gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}\partial_W\\\partial_\theta\\\partial_\phi\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}
\tan\theta\cos\phi&\tan\theta\sin\phi&1\\
W\sec^2\theta\cos\phi&W\sec^2\theta\sin\phi&0\\
-W\tan\theta\sin\phi&W\tan\theta\cos\phi&0
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\partial_x\\\partial_y\\\partial_z\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
If $J=\partial(x,y,z)/\partial(W,\theta,\phi)$ uses the usual convention of coordinate <functions> as rows, the displayed coefficient matrix is $J^{\mathsf T}$. Its <determinant> is $W^2\sec^2\theta\tan\theta>0$ on the given domain, so the inverse operator transformation exists everywhere there.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For an independent inversion, use the coordinate relations
$$
W=z,\qquad \theta=\arctan\frac{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}z,\qquad
\phi=\arctan\frac yx.
$$
The positive-octant domain fixes their angle branches unambiguously. Let $s=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=W\tan\theta$. Differentiating at fixed Cartesian coordinates gives
$$
\theta_x=\frac{z x}{s(z^2+s^2)}=\frac{\cos^2\theta\cos\phi}{W},\qquad
\theta_y=\frac{\cos^2\theta\sin\phi}{W},\qquad
\theta_z=-\frac{\sin\theta\cos\theta}{W},
$$
and $\phi_x=-y/s^2=-\sin\phi/(W\tan\theta)$, $\phi_y=x/s^2=\cos\phi/(W\tan\theta)$, $\phi_z=0$. Hence the inverse <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{(\partial_x)_{y,z}
=\frac{\cos^2\theta\cos\phi}{W}(\partial_\theta)_{W,\phi}
-\frac{\sin\phi}{W\tan\theta}(\partial_\phi)_{W,\theta},}
$$
$$
\boxed{(\partial_y)_{x,z}
=\frac{\cos^2\theta\sin\phi}{W}(\partial_\theta)_{W,\phi}
+\frac{\cos\phi}{W\tan\theta}(\partial_\phi)_{W,\theta},}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{(\partial_z)_{x,y}=(\partial_W)_{\theta,\phi}
-\frac{\sin\theta\cos\theta}{W}(\partial_\theta)_{W,\phi}.}
$$
Multiplying these coefficients by the forward coefficient matrix gives the identity. The original PDF correctly holds $x,y$ fixed in the final <derivative>; the transcription's $x,z$ subscript there is a typo.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On a connected domain, a <vector field> is a <conservative vector field> if and only if it is the <gradient> of a single-valued <scalar potential>, $\boxed{\mathbf H=\boldsymbol\nabla\phi}$. Equivalently, its <line integral> is path independent, or its integral around every closed curve is zero. For a continuously differentiable field on a <simply connected> domain, this is equivalent to $\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf H=\mathbf0$. Without the domain hypothesis, vanishing <curl> alone is only a local test and is not the necessary-and-sufficient global statement.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Yes. For any sufficiently smooth scalar $\chi$, the <gauge transformation>
$$
\boxed{\widetilde{\mathbf G}=\mathbf G+\boldsymbol\nabla\chi}
$$
leaves its <curl> unchanged because $\boldsymbol\nabla\times\boldsymbol\nabla\chi=\mathbf0$. This freedom is much larger than adding a constant vector: for example, $\chi=xy$ adds the nonconstant vector $(y,x,0)$. Two <vector potentials> for the same field differ by a curl-free field; on a <simply connected> domain that difference is a <gradient>, while nontrivial topology may allow additional global freedom.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take the <curl> component by component:
$$
\mathbf F=\left(\partial_y\frac{y^3}{3}-\partial_z\frac{x^3}{3},\partial_z\frac{z^3}{3}-\partial_x\frac{y^3}{3},\partial_x\frac{x^3}{3}-\partial_y\frac{z^3}{3}\right)
=\boxed{(y^2,z^2,x^2).}
$$
Its <divergence> is zero, as expected because the <divergence of a curl is zero>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a smooth oriented surface $S$ with piecewise smooth boundary $C=\partial S$ and a continuously differentiable <vector field> $\mathbf G$, the <Stokes theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\int_S(\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf G)\cdot\mathbf n\,dS
=\oint_{\partial S}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r.}
$$
The <boundary orientation> is fixed by the <right-hand rule>: curling the fingers along the positive boundary direction makes the thumb point along the surface normal. For an outward upper cap, its boundary <ellipse> is traversed counterclockwise when viewed from the positive $z$ direction. The figure shows two possible caps with the same oriented boundary, anticipating the last part.

\Image[paper-1-stokes-orientation.png]
{title=Two outward-oriented caps share a counterclockwise boundary and the same curl flux}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The integrand field is exactly the <curl> computed in part (a), so use its <polynomial> <vector potential> $\mathbf G=(z^3,x^3,y^3)/3$. The upper cap meets the plane $z=0$ in an <ellipse>. Since the parameters are only assumed nonzero, set $a_0=|a|$ and $b_0=|b|$ to use positive semiaxes. Its correctly oriented boundary is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(a_0\cos t,b_0\sin t,0),\qquad0\leq t\leq2\pi.
$$
On that boundary, $G_x=0$ and $dz=0$, so only $G_y\,dy=x^3dy/3$ contributes. The <Stokes theorem> therefore gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
A&=\oint_C\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r
=\frac13\int_0^{2\pi}(a_0\cos t)^3(b_0\cos t)dt\\
&=\frac{a_0^3b_0}{3}\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^4t\,dt
=\frac{a_0^3b_0}{3}\frac{3\pi}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The last integral follows by expanding $\cos^4t=(3+4\cos2t+\cos4t)/8$. Thus
$$
\boxed{A=\frac\pi4|a|^3|b|=\frac\pi4a^2|ab|.}
$$
The result is independent of $c$. A sign check uses the <divergence theorem>: closing the cap with its base disk adds outward normal $-\widehat{\mathbf z}$ there. Since $\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=0$, the cap <flux> equals $\int_{\rm disk}x^2\,dx\,dy>0$, consistent with the boxed expression and its absolute values.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The modified upper surface has exactly the same <ellipse> at $z=0$ and the same <boundary orientation>. The field retains the global <polynomial> <vector potential> from part (a). By the <Stokes theorem>, both surface fluxes equal the same boundary <line integral>; the cap's height and interior shape do not enter. Hence
$$
\boxed{A=\frac\pi4a^2|ab|.}
$$
This is an instance of the fact that <solenoidal flux depends on the oriented boundary>, with the domain condition automatically satisfied by the smooth <polynomial> field on all of $\mathbb R^3$. When $a,b$ are chosen positive, the formula simplifies to $\pi a^3b/4$; the absolute-value form respects the original allowance of arbitrary nonzero signs.
